\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia 2nd Fin Min: "Very Happy" About Fund Inflows  \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2007\par 05:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Malaysia is "very happy" with the recent inflow of foreign funds into the country, Second Finance Minister Nor Mohamed Yakcop said Tuesday, a day after Asian central bankers warned that rising regional capital flows pose a major challenge.  \par \par \uc2 "No. We are not concerned at all, we are very happy with the capital inflows," he told reporters when asked whether the government was concerned about the inflows. "We will carry on with the policy to encourage portfolio inflows - that is the policy."  \par \par \uc2 Asian central bankers meeting in Tokyo Monday said that large capital inflows into the region posed a policy challenge because of its impact on economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, apparently to smoothen rising capital flows after Thailand recently introduced capital controls.  \par \par \uc2 The second finance minister also reiterated that the government is confident that last year's gross domestic product growth probably exceeded the government's 5.8% target. The economy expanded by 6% in the first three quarters.  \par \par \uc2 -By E-Ling Liaw, Dow Jones Newswires; (603) 2692 5254, e-ling.liaw\u174\'00\'AEdowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ven Ram [ 23-01-07 0452GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070123e31n0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
