\par\par \b \uc2 US Treasury:China Moving To Flexible Yuan But Too Slowly  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 2,119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2006\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. Treasury Department on Tuesday again declined to designate China as guilty of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in a report, noting the government in Beijing is moving steadily, though slowly, toward a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 "The Department of Treasury concluded that no major trading partner of the U.S. met the technical requirements for designation," according to the closely watched and overdue report to Congress on international exchange rate policies during the first half of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury outlined steps China is taking to loosen restrictions on capital moving in and out of the country and develop new financial instruments, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures, to prepare for a freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In recent months, the government has allowed the value of the Chinese yuan to fluctuate more and the pace of appreciation has picked up. This progress made it difficult to say that China is managing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime with the intent of gaining a trade advantage, a senior Treasury official told reporters on condition of anonymity.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, China is moving too cautiously and the pace of reforms are too slow, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 "China's cautious approach to exchange rate reform continues to exacerbate distortions in the domestic economy and impede adjustment of international imbalances," Treasury said. "Clamping down on exchange rate fluctuations has increased pressures in other parts of the economy."  \par \par \uc2 Treasury's decision, originally due Oct. 15, was widely expected. The department has refused to call China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator in recent reports. And since the last report, released in May, China has allowed the yuan to appreciate more rapidly. The yuan strengthened 0.5% against the U.S. dollar in the second half of 2005 and another 1.0% in the first half 2006. Since China abandoned the dollar peg in July 2005, the yuan has risen just under 6.0%.  \par \par \uc2 This is far too little, say U.S. manufacturers and their representatives in Congress, who argue the yuan is undervalued by as much as 40%. Indeed, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to accumulate massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent faster yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury will be under fire from those critics, who say the Bush administration hasn't been tough enough on China, when Congress returns for a new session in January. They got a boost last week when Federal Reserve Board Chairman Ben Bernanke, in the prepared text of a speech, called the undervalued yuan an export "subsidy."  \par \par \uc2 Members from both the House and the Senate are working on legislation to overhaul the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporting requirement and develop new laws, mostly directed at China, to pressure countries that limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. A measure backed by Sens. Max Baucus, D-Mont., and Charles Grassley, R-Iowa, would target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "misalignment" rather than "manipulation," taking the question of intent out of consideration of whether Treasury should call out a country for problematic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury officials have said repeatedly that they believe diplomacy will be far more effective in getting China to reform than trade sanctions. Last week, U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson led a delegation including almost half of President George W. Bush's cabinet for talks with senior Chinese officials on economic relations between the two countries.  \par \par \uc2 The meeting yielded some modest agreements, such as for sharing environmental and energy technology and pledges to work on an agreement protecting investment flows. At the close of the meeting, Paulson said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will remain "at the core" of U.S.-China engagement.  \par \par \uc2 In the report released Tuesday, Treasury noted China's average monthly accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was $20.4 billion in the first half this year. That measure fell to $15.6 billion in the third quarter, coinciding with a pick-up in the pace of yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The burden of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hindered its ability to control interest rates and inflation, Treasury said. It has also impeded rebalancing of Chinese growth away from investment and exports in favor of more consumption, a stated goal of the Chinese government.  \par \par \uc2 For its part, the U.S. should help reduce global trade imbalances, which economists warn could spark financial market instability, by encouraging its citizens to save more and by cutting budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Turning to other trading partners, Treasury said tepid economic growth in Europe and Japan has contributed to global trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "Europe and Japan each need to achieve higher rates of growth of domestic demand on a sustained basis," Treasury said, noting that it is uncertain whether Europe's brighter economic outlook will last. Growth in Japan is already decelerating from last year's recovery, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 Most important U.S. trading partners have floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes in place. Japan, whose trade-weighted yen is at its weakest level in two decades, hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  - noted in previous Treasury reports for its large and growing current account surplus - like other oil-exporting countries would benefit from more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 Trade surpluses among major oil exporters are now outpacing those of Southeast Asia's developing economies, inflated by higher oil prices. Russia's current account surplus totaled $56.5 billion in the first half 2006, or equal to just over 11% of gross domestic product. Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the pace of ruble appreciation against the dollar, 6% in the first half 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia, like other Gulf countries, pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar. Between 2004 and 2006, Saudi's oil revenue almost doubled to just under $200 billion. A portion of this money returns to the U.S. for investment, raising the U.S. capital account surplus, a mirror image of the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury said the oil producers could help sustain global economic growth and reduce global trade imbalances by investing more in oil production capacity, investing more in their economies, and moving to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \p