e dollar, which has gained 6% in the first half of 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saudi Arabia, like other Gulf countries, pegs its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the U.S. dollar. Between 2004 and 2006, Saudi's oil revenue almost doubled to just under $200 billion. A portion of this money returns to the U.S. for investment, raising the U.S. capital account surplus, a mirror image of the current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury said the oil producers could help sustain global economic growth and reduce global trade imbalances by investing more in oil production capacity, investing more in their economies and moving to a more flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com [ 19-12-06 2153GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061219e2cj0017w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
