\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW: Malaysia To Moderate Major  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves As Needed  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hasan Jafri OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to moderate any major outflows, particularly if such moves don't reflect economic fundamentals, Bank Negara Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said.  \par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in a strengthening trend, it has in the past been susceptible to outflows of speculative money.  \par \par \uc2 'We would moderate any excessive movement, especially if those movements are being determined by short term flows. Such excessive movements would not reflect the fundamentals of the economy,' Zeti told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview when asked how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would react if the ringgit comes under downward pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Her comments follow a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report Wednesday, which signaled that Bank Negara will allow 'market conditions' as well as economic fundamentals to determine the value of the ringgit, now under a managed-float regime after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July dismantled a peg against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants read the comment to mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now comfortable with a stronger ringgit, which has gained 2.5% this year after appreciating just 0.5% from July to December 2005.  \par \par \uc2 At 0146 GMT, the dollar was quoted at MYR3.6930.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the overall gain in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the peg was dismantled, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves dropped sharply late last year as speculators sold short term securities, with some MYR20 billion leaving the country in September to November before Bank Negara raised the overnight policy rate for the first time in seven years.  \par \par \uc2 Data released this week show a net outflow of MYR11.9 billion in Malaysian short-term money market and debt securities in 2005 compared with an inflow of MYR33.8 billion in 2004 when there was anticipation that the ringgit would gain after it was unpegged from the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On the economy, Zeti said Malaysia's external sector would be a key support for growth in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 'The external sector is going to do better,' she said. 'The external environment has become more favorable in 2006. If you look at world trade, it is projected to increase from 7% to 7.4%. We expect the external sector to do very much better than in 2005.'  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara expects Malaysia's gross domestic product to grow by 6% this year, faster than the 5.3% expansion last year and despite a likely moderation in domestic demand and private-sector consumption. -By Hasan Jafri, Dow Jones Newswires; 6032692 5254; hasan.jafri@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060324e23o0035y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
