\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysian Ringgit Up Sharply Late; Govt Bonds Tad Higher  \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--The Malaysian ringgit soared late Wednesday on local corporate and foreign speculative buying, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Some local exporters may be buying ringgit to pay taxes," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners also bought the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations it will continue to strengthen, in line with gains in the Chinese yuan, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday, the yuan ended at another post-revaluation high after the U.S. again called on China to allow greater movement in the yuan and introduce more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. The U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0369, compared with CNY8.0403 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has the potential to fall below MYR3.7000 to MYR3.6935 by late April if China allows the dollar to fall to CNY8.00, Singapore-based DBS Bank said in a report. So far this year, the ringgit has risen 1.9% against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday, traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  through agent banks to slow the ringgit's rise was muted. "I think they may have given up because the (dollar) selling is quite heavy," said the local-bank trader.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market via agent banks, traders say. It doesn't typically comment on its market activities.  \par \par \uc2 At 0830 GMT, the dollar was at MYR3.7090, its lowest since the ringgit's unpegging July 21, after trading between MYR3.7090 and MYR3.7130. Late Tuesday, the dollar was at MYR3.7125.  \par \par \uc2 Non-deliverable forwards fell in tandem with the lower dollar in the spot market. The one-month rate was MYR3.7000, down from Tuesday's MYR3.7030, while the three-month rate was MYR3.6850, less than the MYR3.6880 seen Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were slightly higher as foreigners bought the paper to park their ringgit holdings.  \par \par \uc2 Still, traders don't expect bonds to rise much in the near term because Tuesday's surprisingly steep retail fuel price increase will prod inflation higher.  \par \par \uc2 "I reckon another 25 basis points (in rate increase) will happen in the second quarter," said a bond trader.  \par \par \uc2 The most actively traded April 2011 Malaysian Government Securities yielded 3.73%, down one basis point, with MYR720 million transacted.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Type of Instrument      Yield    Change from \par                                     Prev Close \par    April 2011 MGS          3.73%    Down 1 bp \par    Overnight money rate    3.20%    Up 2 bps \par    3-month Klibor          3.45%    Down 1 bp \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Carolyn Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; carolyn.lim@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 01-03-06 0907GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1026 1851  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060301e231000ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
