\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia Ringgit Up Late At New Recent Peak; Bonds Flat  \b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2006\par 09:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--The ringgit rose late Thursday to a fresh post-unpegging high on expectations of further gains in the Chinese yuan and in reaction to a 25-basis-point hike in Malaysia's key policy rate, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a rumor about the yuan going upward. That's why the dollar has become well-offered," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Traders ignored remarks by U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs Tim Adams, who urged more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in Malaysia.  \par \par \uc2 "We compliment the Malaysian government for making a switch to a different regime. We'd like to see some more flexibility," Adams said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0745 GMT, the dollar was quoted, but not traded, at MYR3.7165, lower than late Wednesday's MYR3.7220. It traded between MYR3.7170 - the lowest for the dollar since July 21 - and MYR3.7215 Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders predict the dollar will fall to MYR3.7150 in the near term as they don't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily enough to reverse the U.S. unit's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, "the agent banks were taking (offers) around MYR3.7170 but they didn't buy much," said the trader at the local bank. This suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted only to smooth price movements, rather than drive the dollar higher, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through agent banks, traders said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't typically comment on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market activities.  \par \par \uc2 Non-deliverable forwards fell late Thursday, in line with the dollar's fall on the spot market. The one-month rate was MYR3.7100, down from Wednesday's MYR3.7130. The three-month rate was MYR3.6930, down from MYR3.6960 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were flat, trimming earlier losses that came in reaction to Bank Negara's raising of its overnight policy rate to 3.25% from 3%.  \par \par \uc2 "There was some selling initially but the market became biddish very quickly," said a bond trader.  \par \par \uc2 He attributed the steady market to hopes Wednesday's rate increase won't happen again any time soon.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a short supply of bonds and with this rate hike we no longer have a problem with negative interest rates," he said. Malaysia's inflation was 3.2% in January.  \par \par \uc2 Consequently, the bond market believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have any need in the short term to raise rates again, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The yield of the April 2011 Malaysian Government Securities was flat at 3.74% late Thursday, after rising as high as 3.80% in the morning and trading as low as 3.73%. Volume traded was a relatively high MYR960 million.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Type of Instrument           Yield         Change from \par                                               Prev Close \par    April 2011 MGS                3.74%        Unchanged \par    Overnight money rate          3.25%        Up 33 bps \par    3-month Klibor                3.44%        Up 16 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Carolyn Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; carolyn.lim@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 23-02-06 0827GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1026 1851  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060223e22n000f7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
