\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Undermined by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  worry  \b0\par\par\uc2 565 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2006\par 10:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 10 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped in holiday-thinned trading on Tuesday, weighed down by profit-taking after their sharp rises in the past week and the anxiety about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  Markets in Singapore, Indonesia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  were closed for a Muslim festival holiday. That kept trading volumes thin.  \par \par \uc2  The won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> closed local trade around 982 per dollar, just off Monday's high of 975 which was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strongest level since the Asian financial crisis in 1997.  \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> fell to 52.645 per dollar, off Monday's peak of 52.25 which was its highest level since May 2003.  \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=> gave up some ground, easing to 39.93 from Monday's 8-month high of 39.69 a dollar.  \par \par \uc2  "Seems like some players took profit, but when we were back up above 39.90 some exporters came to sell dollars," said a baht trader based in Bangkok.  \par \par \uc2  Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped as the dollar staged a slight rebound from three-month lows struck the past couple of sessions against the euro <EUR=> and yen <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2  Also, markets paused to see the extent to which central banks around the region would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have risen sharply since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2  South Korean authorities kept up their normal run of comments.  \par \par \uc2  The Vice Finance Minister said the won had risen too fast and steps would be taken as necessary, soon after a Bank of Korea official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen above an equilibrium range against the dollar but authorities would not force it lower.  \par \par \uc2  "Korean authorities clearly are not comfortable with either the pace of the move or the rate of the won," Richard Yetsenga, HSBC's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said.  \par \par \uc2  "But it unlikely that they will be successful in preventing the won moving at all."  \par \par \uc2  Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN AMRO Bank, said in a note the comments suggested there would be no concerted attempt to cap the won.  \par \par \uc2  "This backs our view that strong growth means Korea will not want to risk the liquidity headaches implied by concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2006 will be tactical rather than  strategic," he said.  \par \par \uc2  That view would be confirmed if the Bank of Korea raised interest rates at its policy meeting on Thursday, he said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0829 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.21         114.20       -0.01\par  Taiwan dlr      32.016         31.942       -0.23\par  Korean won      981.80         977.40       -0.45\par  Baht             39.85          39.78       -0.18\par  Peso             52.65          52.47       -0.34\par  Rupee            44.32          44.31       -0.02\par  Yuan            8.0657         8.0641       -0.02\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlig