\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia's Zeti: Rate Increase Likely If 3Q GDP Improves  \b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2005\par 01:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)-Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise interest rates if third-quarter economic data improve over the second quarter, Bank Negara Malaysia Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz hinted over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "If growth improves in the third quarter, we can expect a monetary policy response," Zeti told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia's second-quarter gross domestic product growth was 4.1% on year. Most economists expect third-quarter growth to come in at around 4.6%. Third-quarter data may be released as early as this week.  \par \par \uc2 "In the second quarter, we saw slower growth amid an environment of rising prices, and at that time the risk of slower growth and higher inflation was about in balance," Zeti said.  \par \par \uc2 Zeti added that the third quarter's performance will give a clearer picture of underlying growth potential and the degree of inflationary conditions.  \par \par \uc2 "The future direction of interest rates would thus depend on whether the risk of slower growth diminishes while the current inflationary trend continues," Zeti said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia has said that inflation is likely to remain above 3% until April of 2006, after rising to 3.7% in August, the highest level since 1999.  \par \par \uc2 And economists are eyeing a possible increase in fuel prices, as the government cuts back on fuel subsidies next year to rein in fiscal spending. In addition, observers say the government could grant national utility Tenaga Nasional Bhd. leeway to increase electricity rates, adding upward pressure to prices.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia has kept its key overnight policy rate steady at 2.70% since its introductin in April 2004, when it replaced the previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy rate.  \par \par \uc2 Economists are upbeat about \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth outlook for the third quarter due to a recovery in cyclical demand for electronics, higher exports, and strengthening industrial output.  \par \par \uc2 The government has said the economy will expand by around 5% this year, after achieving 5.8% on year growth in the first quarter, followed by 4.1% in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 In mid-November, the Malaysia-based U.S. electronics manufacturers' group said third-quarter export sales rose 14.6% on year to MYR18.96 billion. Exports from this industry group accounted for around 19% of Malaysia's total manufactured exports in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysian exports as a whole rose 7.4% on year in September after a surprisingly strong 12.5% jump in August.  \par \par \uc2 Most observers expect a gradual increase of rates at between 25 to 30 basis points, but have been divided over whether the timing of the move will be this year or next.  \par \par \uc2 "We think Bank Negara may...go ahead with a 30 basis point rate hike...," Chua Hak Bin, an economist at DBS Economic Market Research, said in a research note last week, citing an acceleration of capital outflows.  \par \par \uc2 "We think the capital outflows represent more than simply foreign funds exiting, and include domestic funds capitalizing on higher interest rates abroad and the recent liberalization of capital controls," Chua added.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia is gradually adopting greater flexibility in its exchange rate regime after removing the ringgit's $1=MYR3.80 peg to the dollar earlier this year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, in addition to restrictions on domestic investment abroad, were put into place in September 1998 and were aimed at limiting capital outflows in the wake of the Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, Fitch Ratings affirmed Malaysia's A- long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating.  \par \par \uc2 "Fitch's rating balances Malaysia's robust international liquidity position and declining external debt profile against the scope for further improvements in public finances," Ai Ling Ngiam, associate director at Fitch's sovereigns team in Asia said Friday. She emphasized Malaysia's external finances as a fundamental rating strength and said Malaysia's current account position will likely remain above 12% of GDP this year.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch also said it expects Malaysia's economic growth to be around 4.9% this year, rising to 5.3% in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 -By Monica Houston-Waesch; Dow Jones Newswires; +49 69 29 725 520; nikki.houston@dowjones.com [ 21-11-05 0000GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051121e1bl00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
