\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia ringgit quoted strongest since peg scrapped  \b0\par\par\uc2 251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2005\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit was quoted on Wednesday at its strongest level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar for the first time in seven years on July 21.  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> was bid as strong as 3.7433 per dollar, Reuters data showed. That was the firmest level since Malaysia ended the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg at 3.8 ringgit to the dollar following China's move to revalue the yuan by 2.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "People have been bidding dollar-ringgit lower but no deals were done at those levels," said a Kuala Lumpur \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer, who said the strongest the ringgit was dealt on Wednesday was at 3.7450 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Ringgit should strengthen very little over the next month," the dealer said, pointing to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Following \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' decision to abandon the dollar-peg, the ringgit initially gained about 1.3 percent in the first three days but has since hovered around 3.75 per dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars to prevent further gains.  \par \par \uc2 Offshore non-deliverable forward contracts showed investors expected the ringgit to strengthen to 3.7380 per dollar in a month and to 3.6530 per dollar in a year, according to Prebon Yamane prices.  \par \par \uc2 That implied a 2.5 percent appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MALAYSIA-RINGGIT|LANGEN|ABN