\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysian ringgit inches higher, forwards up  \b0\par\par\uc2 260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2005\par 05:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 2 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit edged up slightly against the dollar on Tuesday, in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that were buoyed by stock market inflows, and the offshore forwards market priced in a further slight appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> was quoted at 3.7455/65 per dollar at 0315 GMT, slightly higher than the 3.7490 quoted at the close of Asian trading on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been quoted at 3.74 overnight.  \par \par \uc2 "Bank Negara should be monitoring the market," said one Kuala Lumpur-based ringgit trader, confirming that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended a seven-year system of pegging the ringgit at 3.8 to the U.S. dollar and floated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on July 21. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 1.5 percent since then.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the country may have received foreign fund inflows of between $5 billion and $10 billion since the revaluation from speculators betting the ringgit will rise.  \par \par \uc2 They reckon the Malaysian authorities will be happy to let the ringgit rise gradually to lower the imported cost of oil. Oil prices hit a record high <CLc1> on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In the offshore non-deliverable ringgit forwards (NDFs) market, the ringgit was priced just a quarter of a percent higher at 3.735 per dollar in a month and at 3.655 in a year, 2.4 percent higher than the current spot rate.  \par 