\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Malaysia Anwar: New Ringgit Regime Like A Peg  \b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2005\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Malaysia's former Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim Friday said Kuala Lumpur's move last week to abandon its peg to the dollar and move to a managed float was basically a peg under a different name, though he welcomed the new regime.  \par \par \uc2 The former finance minister, who was sacked a day after then-Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad imposed the peg in 1998 as part of capital controls during the Asian financial crisis, also reiterated his call for a free float of the ringgit to prevent speculation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to make the economy more competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Similar to a move by China last week, Malaysia delinked the ringgit, which had been fixed at 3.80 to the dollar since September 1998 and was widely viewed as undervalued. Since the move, the ringgit has risen about 1.3% against the dollar but has stayed relatively stable since Tuesday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the relatively slight strengthening was largely due to aggressive dollar buying by the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara.  \par \par \uc2 "Notwithstanding that they were compelled to change after the revaluation of the yuan, I would certainly see this as a positive move," he told Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC Asia-Pacific in an interview. CNBC will broadcast portions of the interview Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But "when you look at the details - the devil is in the details - substantially nothing has changed," he said. "What they would call a managed float is actually a re-peg and it's unfortunate that the band or latitude is so limited to the extent that Bank Negara has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately after the so-called unpegging."  \par \par \uc2 Anwar said \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "adequate enough protection" - possibly referring to the nation's $75 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and strong growth - to fully float the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though he said there would be some initial volatility.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Foreigners Delaying Investment \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A former Mahathir protege, Anwar has been critical of the policies of current Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi and has said the tight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls have caused Malaysia to lose its attractiveness for foreign investment and its competitiveness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The medium- to long-term sustainability of an economy is a crucial determinant of where multinational corporations invest, he said, saying Malaysian leaders must stop blaming China and India for siphoning off investment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That is why I have called on the prime minister, who is also the minister of finance, to take a more decisive position vis-a-vis the pegging issue," he said. "People invest in a big way when they are confident about the sustainability."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before Kuala Lumpur's decision last week, the Malaysian Institute of Economic Research had said the country's large trade and current account surpluses suggested a serious exchange rate misalignment, and warned the peg was already hurting the economy as seen in large speculative inflows in the money market, a soaring import bill and rising inflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It noted some foreign investors were delaying investments pending anticipated exchange rate changes and warned that competitiveness based on a cheap \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can't be sustained.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anwar was sacked and arrested in 1998 after a fallout with Mahathir and was later convicted and sentenced to 15 years in jail for corruption and sodomy. But Malaysia's highest court overturned the sodomy conviction last September and freed Anwar because he had completed the corruption sentence. But the politician is barred from active politics until 2008 under rules regarding those who have been convicted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He says he was framed in a conspiracy to block him from challenging Mahathir for power. Mahathir, who retired in October 2003, has denied plotting against his former deputy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anwar accused Mahathir of imposing the peg at the time to "protect the interest of his cronies and companies" mired in heavy debt.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He was in Tokyo to speak at a United Nations University conference and to meet with the media and politicians, including the head of Japan's largest opposition party, Katsuya Okada of the Democratic Party. Anwar currently is a visiting fellow at the School of Advanced International Studies at Johns Hopkins University in Washington DC.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kenneth McCallum and Hugh Lawson [ 29-07-05 0730GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050729e17t000cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
