\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  speculators buy up Malaysian bonds  \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2005\par 05:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR, July 25 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators dived into Malaysia's bond market on Monday, betting on further strengthening of the ringgit after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar was severed last week, bond traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone believes that the ringgit is going to appreciate," a bond trader with a local bank said, as bond prices rose across the board, four days after Malaysia scrapped the seven-year-old peg and followed China in moving to a managed-float \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "It's going to lead to more speculation," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The yield curve on Malaysian government paper took a step down, with the yield on the five-year bond due February 2009 falling to a new all-time low of 3.13 percent, and the benchmark 3-year bond down seven basis points to yield 3.05 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are buying in anticipation of further strengthening in the ringgit. We see more downside to yields in the near term," said a dealer with another local bank.  \par \par \uc2 But local share prices fell, with most speculative capital heading for the government debt market and many local share investors taking profits after the benchmark Composite Index <.KLSE> hit a five-year closing high on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "Foreign hedge funds are taking a three-to-six month view, where they are less likely to lose money on the fixed-income side, especially during a short term period," said a fund manager with a leading local asset management firm in Kuala Lumpur.  \par \par \uc2 "Their principal will be protected, and (the hope is) they will just make on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Whereas in equities it'll be a hell of a ride, for example in a 2-3 percent move either way."  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit, fixed for seven years at 3.8 to the dollar until last Thursday night, is widely viewed by economists to be about 10 percent undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 Since Malaysia switched to a managed float, minutes after China made a similar move with the yuan, the ringgit has strengthened by about 1 percent against the dollar.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent a rapid appreciation, but local bond dealers said the ringgit would find it difficult to resist further strengthening as long as the yuan continued to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 "My own personal view is they will perhaps let it go to the level that we have seen the yuan appreciate, which is about 2 percent," HCBC Malaysia treasurer John McGowan said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will use a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a guide to managing the ringgit, but declines to give details on its new policy, other than to say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already around fair value at the old, fixed rate of 3.8.  \par \par \uc2 "They say we are tied to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but as I see it we are tied to the yuan," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Hsu Chuang Khoo and Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MALAYSIA|LANGEN|SI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050725e17p0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
