\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-UPDATE 3-Malaysia cenbank curbs  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  after dropping peg  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Liau Y-Sing and Hsu Chuang Khoo  \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds dropped work in first paragraph)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR, July 22 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit rose on Friday after the government abandoned a policy of fixing the exchange rate with the dollar, but it defied predictions of sharp gains on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped the seven-year old peg of 3.8 per dollar and followed China's move to a managed float, but dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent heavily on the first day of trading on Friday to keep a lid on speculative buying of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They have been there all morning, bidding at 3.795," a trader said, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought at least $1 billion against the ringgit, which is viewed as undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been ploughing money into Malaysian bonds and stocks over the past year, betting the ringgit would be revalued once China relaxed its policies to allow the yuan to rise.  \par \par \uc2 They were right on the chain of events -- Malaysia moved immediately after Beijing late on Thursday -- but Friday's ringgit gains were not enough to spark profit-taking and a flight of capital, which had been a key concern facing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=>, still banned from offshore trade, firmed by about 0.7 percent to trade late in the day at 3.7750 per dollar, an intraday high.  \par \par \uc2 But derivatives markets offshore that settle only in U.S. dollars were factoring in an 8 percent rise in the value of the ringgit over the next year.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said a firmer ringgit can help curb inflation, which hit a 6-year high in June, and keep interest rates low.  \par \par \uc2 But speculators could continue to pile potentially destabilising funds into the country if they believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that the ringgit has appreciated so little from its original rate could attract more speculative inflows as investors bet on continued appreciation in coming weeks," said Sani Hamid, economist with Singapore's Forecast Private Ltd..  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent to 8.11 per dollar on Thursday from 8.28 per dollar. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell back slightly to 8.1111 per dollar in trading on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Malaysian treasury bills tumbled on Friday, partly as foreign investors bought bonds. Yielding less than U.S. treasuries, they offer little attraction to foreigners apart from potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, economists said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing some further interest from the offshore in our domestic treasury bills," HSBC Malaysia treasurer John McGowan said. "My own personal view is that it (ringgit) will gradually appreciate on an ongoing basis, from day to day," he added.  \par \par \uc2 'READY FOR ANYTHING'  \par \par \uc2 With a war-chest of $75.2 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered well armed to deal with existing speculators.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia's prime minister vowed late on Thursday the country was "ready for anything" speculators could throw at the ringgit.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters, probably the biggest beneficiaries of an undervalued ringgit, are unlikely to be badly hurt, since cheaper imports can reduce their costs, economists said.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysian exporters rely heavily on imported raw material. Electronics, which make up about half of total exports, have an import content of about 80 percent, economists say.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in exporters, from electronics to oil-palm plantation firms, fell in early trade on Friday, but partially recovered as it became clear the ringgit was standing its ground.  \par \par \uc2 But overall, foreign investors, who had long chafed against the ringgit peg and related capital controls, applauded the move and helped push the stock market <.KLSE> to close at a six-month high.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysian Pacific Industries Bhd , the nation's biggest chip maker, and Kuala Lumpur Kepong Bhd, an oil palm planter, recovered from early losses to close 2.5 percent and 1.4 percent down, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 Companies with big foreign debts to service, such as power utility Tenaga Nasional Bhd , would benefit. Tenaga rose 4.6 percent on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Mark Bendeich and Barani Krishnan in Kuala Lumpur and Dhara Ranasinghe and Vidya Ranganathan in Singapore)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-MALAYSIA (UPDATE 3, PICTURE, REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050722e17m0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
