\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:USD/MYR Falls, Short-End  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2  Paper Soars   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Lim   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,022 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 12:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the continued exclusion of offshore players Friday helped restrain the dollar's fall against the ringgit, a day after the country embraced a managed float system and dropped its 7-year-old fixed-rate regime.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in (the market) since this morning," said a trader at a foreign bank. "It's just an onshore market so activity will be limited."   \par \par \uc2 The Association of Banks Malaysia, an industry grouping, late Friday echoed this sentiment, saying, "...as the ringgit remains non-tradeable offshore, this would allow Bank Negara to maintain a tightly managed band for the ringgit."   \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, the dollar was bid as low as MYR3.7700 compared with the previous MYR3.8000 fixed rate, which was abandoned in lockstep with China's yuan revaluation. Late in the day, the dollar trimmed its losses and was bid at MYR3.7750.   \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara said late Thursday the ringgit would be allowed to operate on a managed-float basis, with economic fundamentals determining its value. "Bank Negara Malaysia will monitor the exchange rate against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket to ensure that the exchange rate remains close to its fair value," the bank said. Prior to this, the ringgit had been fixed at MYR3.80 to a dollar since September 1998.   \par \par \uc2 Friday, Bank Negara declined to comment on whether it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars earlier in the day via several banks, but was largely absent by the afternoon.   \par \par \uc2 In its regime change statement Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't specify a particular value for the ringgit, but the Association of Banks expects the ringgit to rise about 2% against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "We expect the ringgit to appreciate in tandem with the yuan, i.e. by about 2.0% against the U.S. dollar," the industry group said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds trimmed losses after earlier plunging in a kneejerk reaction to the ringgit regime change. The early weakness in bonds was due to fears the change could prompt a reversal of foreign funds that have flowed into the market since late 2004, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Short-end debt has since recovered, with most tenors closing higher compared with Thursday, on renewed foreign buying. Some speculators set up new positions on hopes of more ringgit appreciation going forward, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 "Someone must be buying heavily. Six- and three-month government paper are yielding 1%," said a trader at a local bank. Six- and three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paper had been yielding 2.36% Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs Regional Economist Adam LeMesurier warned that the inflows could spark the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wrath.   \par \par \uc2 "We would watch for any potential Bank Negara moral suasion to the local banks against intermediating foreign inflows into the domestic bond market," LeMesurier said in a research report.   \par \par \uc2 "This could be a sign that Bank Negara is becoming upset with the level of inflows," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the key composite stock index soared 1.9% to end at 969.69, just shy of the year's high of 940.94 achieved in January. The market's biggest stocks in capitalization terms - Malayan Banking (1155.KU), Telekom Malaysia (4863.KU), and power utility Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU) - all benefitted handsomely from renewed optimism after the peg's removal. Maybank surged 4.5% to MYR11.60, Telekom rallied 5.7% to MYR11.20 and Tenaga gained 4.6% to MYR11.30.   \par \par \uc2 Mayban Securities economist Saifuddin Morat attributed the market's initial surge to the "feel-good factor" from the regime change.   \par \par \uc2 "With the ringgit peg removed, the equity market's greater attractiveness to foreign funds means that the KLCI could trade at the higher range of our target of between 940 and 970 for this year," Saifuddin said.   \par \par \uc2 Still, analysts and traders are split over the medium-term impact of the regime change on the dollar/ringgit rate.   \par \par \uc2 "We will expect the initial value to overshoot, but thereafter for the (dollar) to settle around MYR3.50," said Standard Chartered Bank economist Joseph Tan.   \par \par \uc2 DBS Bank's Chua Hak Bin thinks the ringgit's rise will be modest initially. "We expect the ringgit to appreciate by only about 2% or 3% in the immediate term," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "An overly sharp appreciation may lead to a massive sell-down of Malaysian assets," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Citigroup's Sim Moh Siong also envisages a slim ringgit rise over the next few months, though bigger gains could come later. "The strengthening of the ringgit by the year-end is likely to be less than 5% and is predicated on a likely appreciation of the Chinese yuan by a total 5% by year-end," Sim said in a report.   \par \par \uc2 "Given the substantial scope for yuan appreciation over the next few years, this could open up more room for ringgit appreciation over the medium term," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Sim is also optimistic that Malaysia won't be hit by massive capital outflows in the wake of the much-speculated-on removal of the peg.   \par \par \uc2 CIMB bond analyst Jason Wong has the same view. "We are not expecting any huge outflow of offshore funds at the moment given that the appreciation of the ringgit hasn't been significant."   \par \par \uc2 Offshore players will hang onto paper with tenors of less than a year, as "the (current slight) appreciation of the ringgit doesn't warrant a sell at the moment, and as the duration (of these papers) is short, any interest rate increase will have minimal impact," Wong said.   \par \par \uc2 February 2009 1/2004 Malaysian Government Securities were at 103.65, above the intraday low of 102.50 and Thursday's 103.52 close.   \par \par \uc2 The September 2015 2/2005 MGS was at 104.40, recovered from the 103.00 low reached earlier Friday, but lower than Thursday's 104.85.   \par \par \uc2 -By Carolyn Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; carolyn.lim@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Joseph Edwin contributed to the story)   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by E. Beiser [ 22-07-05 1046GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050722e17m000i9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
