\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: USD/MYR Falls, Long-End  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2  Bonds Slide   \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the continued exclusion of offshore players Friday helped keep the dollar's fall against the ringgit modest a day after the country embraced a managed float system and dropped its 7-year-old fixed-rate regime.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in since this morning," said a trader at a foreign bank. "It's just an onshore market so activity will be limited."   \par \par \uc2 So far, the dollar has fallen a mere 0.8% to MYR3.7700-MYR3.7800 from the previous MYR3.8000 fixed rate that was abandoned in lockstep with China's yuan revaluation.   \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, Bank Negara said the ringgit would be allowed to operate on a managed float basis with economic fundamentals determining its value. "Bank Negara Malaysia will monitor the exchange rate against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket to ensure that the exchange rate remains close to its fair value," it said.   \par \par \uc2 On Friday, Bank Negara declined to comment on whether it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Short-end government bonds plunged early Friday before recovering, while longer-end issues fell in the afternoon after being relatively steady in the morning. The weakness in bonds was due to fears the ringgit regime change could prompt a reversal of foreign funds that have flowed into the market since late 2004, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the key composite stock index jumped 1.8% to 938.69 at its open before trimming its gains midmorning. The market's biggest stocks in capitalization terms - Malayan Banking (1155.KU), Telekom Malaysia (4863.KU), and power utility Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU) - benefitted from renewed optimism after the peg's removal. At 0730 GMT, the Kuala Lumpur Composite Index was up 1.1% at 932.27 as the market had stabilized, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Mayban Securities economist Saifuddin Morat attributed the market's initial surge to the "feel-good factor" from the regime change.   \par \par \uc2 "With the ringgit peg removed, the equity market's greater attractiveness to foreign funds means that the KLCI could trade at the higher range of our target of between 940 and 970 for this year," Saifuddin said.   \par \par \uc2 Still, analysts and traders are split over the medium-term dollar/ringgit impact of the regime change.   \par \par \uc2 "We will expect the initial value to overshoot, but thereafter for the (dollar) to settle around MYR3.50," said Standard Chartered Bank economist Joseph Tan.   \par \par \uc2 DBS Bank's Chua Hak Bin thinks the ringgit's rise will be modest initially. "We expect the ringgit to appreciate by only about 2% or 3% in the immediate term," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "An overly sharp appreciation may lead to a massive sell-down of Malaysian assets," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Citigroup's Sim Moh Siong also envisages a slim ringgit rise over the next few months. "The strengthening of the ringgit by the year-end is likely to be less than 5% and is predicated on a likely appreciation of the Chinese yuan by a total 5% by year-end," Sim said in a report.   \par \par \uc2 Still, "given the substantial scope for yuan appreciation over the next few years, this could open up more room for ringgit appreciation over the medium term," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Sim is also optimistic that Malaysia won't be hit by massive capital outflows in the wake of the much-speculated-on removal of the peg.   \par \par \uc2 CIMB bond analyst Jason Wong has the same view. "We are not expecting any huge outflow of offshore funds at the moment given that the appreciation of the ringgit hasn't been significant."   \par \par \uc2 Offshore players will hang onto paper with tenors of less than a year as "the (current slight) appreciation of ringgit doesn't warrant a sell at the moment, and as the duration (of these papers) is short, any interest rate increase will have minimal the impact," Wong said.   \par \par \uc2 February 2009 1/2004 Malaysian Government Securities were at 103.55, above the intraday low of 102.50 and Thursday's 103.52 close.   \par \par \uc2 The September 2015 2/2005 MGS was at 103.20, higher than the 103.00 low reached earlier Friday, but lower than Thursday's 104.85.   \par \par \uc2 -By Carolyn Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; carolyn.lim@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Joseph Edwin contributed to the story)   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 22-07-05 0807GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050722e17m000cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
