\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysian rate hikes, peg move seen in 2005 - DBS  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2005\par 13:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR, July 5 (Reuters) - Malaysia is expected to raise interest rates and abandon its ringgit peg this year to fend off rising inflation and close a widening interest rate gap with the United States, Singapore's DBS Bank said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia may lift its overnight policy rate by 25 basis points in the third quarter and another 25 basis points in the fourth quarter, economist Chua Hak Bin said in a report.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight policy rate, Malaysia's benchmark interest rate, has been at 2.70 percent since it was introduced in April 2004. In that time, the U.S. federal funds rates has risen to 3.25 percent from 1.00 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara, Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has said it may keep interest rates low to support growth even if oil prices stay high, but investors expect inflation to lead to tighter policy.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysian inflation has been trending higher, as higher oil prices drive up food and transport costs. Inflation hit 3.1 percent in the year through May, the biggest rise since a 3.2 percent gain in the year through March 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Chua said Malaysia might also abandon its ringgit peg of 3.8 per dollar for a managed float in the second half of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Markets expect a revaluation of China's yuan would trigger a ringgit move, but Chua said this carried a risk for Malaysia.  \par \par \uc2 "Such an event could trigger a wave of speculative capital inflows, requiring huge sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a collapse in interest rates," Chua said.  \par \par \uc2 "We think Bank Negara Malaysia would want to avoid such an ugly scenario, shifting to a managed float before a (yuan) move."  \par \par \uc2 Authorities can issue debt securities to mop up the excess cash, a process known as sterilisation.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation of a revaluation has sucked short-term money into Malaysian bonds and shares, raising concern about destabilising capital flows and possible inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said last week that Malaysia would review its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy when China revalued its yuan.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-MALAYSIA-DBS|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|ABX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050705e175000w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
