\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Lowers Malaysian 2003 GDP Forecast To 4.2% From 5% \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 08:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- The International Monetary Fund has revised down its economic growth forecast for Malaysia this year to 4.2% from a previous estimate of 5% made in April. \par \par \uc2  In its World Economic Outlook report issued Thursday, the IMF also revised down its 2004 forecast to 5.3% from the 5.8% projected in April. \par \par \uc2  The IMF attributed the lower forecasts to weaker domestic demand resulting from the outbreak of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome earlier this year, and from uncertainties related to the Iraq crisis. \par \par \uc2  The IMF expects Malaysia's average inflation to remain modest this year at 1.7%, which is a bit above the government's forecast of 1.1%. \par \par \uc2  "For the most part, the policy response to weaker growth has been appropriate," the IMF stated, referring to the region as a whole. "With inflation contained, monetary policy has been eased in a number of countries, though the continued buildup of official reserves suggest that reforms to increase exchange rate flexibility remained largely on hold." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its key \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate - used by banks to calculate prime lending rates - to 4.5% in May from 5%, as part of an economic stimulus package, which included 1.7 billion ringgit ($1=MYR3.80) in on-budget fiscal expenditures. \par \par \uc2  In its annual economic report issued with the budget last Friday, the finance ministry reiterated that the ringgit's peg to the dollar, currently MYR3.8, that was introduced in 1998 remains supported by economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said the government would only review the peg if there was a structural and fundamental shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions. \par \par \uc2  Observers say the country is highly unlikely to tinker with the peg ahead of the power transition end-October, when Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad hands the scepter to his deputy Abdullah Ahmad Badawi, nor in the near term thereafter. \par \par \uc2  In its report, the IMF reiterated calls on countries to make exchange rates more elastic in order to even out imbalances between countries' economies. \par \par \uc2  "From a policy standpoint, moreover, further exchange rate flexibility would confer a number of benefits - including reducing risks of future crises, making domestic growth less dependent on the vagaries of the global cycle, lower holding costs of official reserves, and raising consumption opportunities for local residents in cases where exchange rates may be undervalued - while also helping to resolve global imbalances," the report said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF report also noted that the accumulation of international reserves in the region in 2002 "was well in excess of that warranted by fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  It also cited the need for current account surpluses in emerging markets in Asia in order to share in the adjustment of the unsustainable U.S. current account deficit "to prevent an undue burden of adjustment in other countries." \par \par \uc2  As at end-August, Malaysia's international reserves stood at $38.7 billion, sufficient to finance six months of retained imports and around 4.3 times short-term external debt. \par \par \uc2  -By Monica Houston-Waesch, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; nikki.houston@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030918dz9i000a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
