\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Malaysia's interest rate hike unlikely till H2 '04. \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2003\par 11:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Malaysia is unlikely to\par tighten monetary policy until the second half of 2004, a\par Reuters poll showed on Wednesday, with inflation in check and\par global interest rates expected to remain low.\par With the U.S. Federal Reserve signalling that it would keep\par interest rates low for a long time, economists saw no immediate\par pressure to lift Malaysian interest rates.\par The poll of 10 economists ruled out a move in the first\par quarter of next year, and on average saw a three percent chance\par of a rate hike in the second quarter.\par A Reuters poll in mid-July predicted that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par Negara would hold rates steady for the rest of 2003.\par "I don't think there'll be any hurry (to increase rates),"\par said David Cohen, director of macro-forecasting at MMS\par International.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The inflation picture is under control and interest rates\par around the world will remain within the low range."\par Malaysia's monthly consumer price index, the official\par barometer of inflation, has stayed below two percent since the\par start of the year.\par As concerns about the domestic economy abate, with growth\par expected to accelerate in the second half of the year, focus\par has turned to a possible hike in borrowing costs.\par The last interest rate movement was in May when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par trimmed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate by 50 basis points to 4.5\par percent.\par Economists saw a relatively bigger probability -- albeit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par only a modest 17 percent chance on average - of a rate hike\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par going into the second half of 2004, as an anticipated global\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic recovery gained ground.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Those who expected a hike saw the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate being\par increased by 25 or 50 basis points.\par The overall chance of an interest rate movement was small,\par economists said, as Malaysia makes few interest rates changes,\par although the ringgit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar.\par The Federal Reserve has cut rates 13 times since January\par 2001, slicing 5.50 percentage points off its key short-term\par interest rate, now at one percent.\par In contrast, Malaysia has only cut rates twice during the\par period -- in September 2001 and May this year.\par Reuters asked 10 economists to estimate the percentage\par chance that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise interest rates in the\par first and second quarters and the second half of 2004.\par Results:\par Pct chance          Pct chance     Pct\par chance\par of Q1 move          of Q2 move     of H2\par move\par AmSecurities                0                   0\par 25\par CIMB Securities             0                   0\par DBS Bank                    0                   0\par 10  HSBC Securities             0                   0\par 0  IDEAglobal.com              0                  25\par 50\par JP Morgan                   0                   0\par Mayban Securities           0                   0\par MMS International           0                   0\par 50\par Standard Chartered          0                   0\par 30  UBS Warburg                 0                   0\par 0\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par - Mean                        0                   3\par 17\par Median  