\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2  Unlikely To Cut  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Rate Soon - Banker \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 11:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to cut its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate any time soon, as lower rates may not necessarily boost lending and spur the local economy, a senior banker said Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have been speculating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon slash the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate - which is used by commercial banks to calculate their own lending rates - to stimulate the economy as prospects of a war in Iraq mount. The rate is currently at 5%, a historical low. \par \par \uc2  However, Bumiputra Commerce Bank Bhd. Executive Vice President Nik Hassan Nik Mohd Amin said such a move was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "A cut won't result in additional investments and it may cause deflation which is more difficult to cure," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Bumiputra-Commerce Bank is a unit of Malaysia's second-biggest financial group Commerce Asset-Holding Bhd. (P.CAH). \par \par \uc2  Nik Hassan said the government's recently announced measures to boost the domestic capital market, coupled with a coming fiscal stimulus package in April, will be sufficient to cushion the economy from a global economic slowdown, should there be war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Amid the global uncertainties, Malaysians are placing their money in banks rather than spending or investing, Nik Hassan noted. \par \par \uc2  Last year, total deposits in banks in the country increased 5.6% or by 2.1 billion ringgit ($1=MYR3.80) from a year earlier, he said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Commerce Asset Director Rozali Mohamed Ali said the group remains optimistic about its financial performance this year despite the risk of war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "We will focus on areas we have identified and hope to continue to perform well this year," Rozali said but declined to elaborate. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Asset is due to announce its 2002 financial results by Friday. Analysts have forecast an average net profit of MYR514.70 million for the year ended Dec. 31, 2002, up 34% from 2001. \par \par \uc2  The release of Commerce Asset's 2002 results has been delayed because of its recent acquisition of Indonesia's PT Bank Niaga. \par \par \uc2  For this year, analysts expect the banking group to post a net profit of around MYR674 million. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Monday, Bumiputra-Commerce Bank signed a $200 million one-year syndicated term loan facility agreement to finance the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its overseas and offshore branches. \par \par \uc2  The group has about MYR6 billion outstanding in U.S. dollar and other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans, Rozali said. \par \par \uc2  -By Joseph Edwin, Dow Jones Newswires; 603 2692 5254; joe.edwin@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Bernice Tang \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030317dz3h000oq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
