\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-INTERVIEW-Sakakibara sees Japan crisis, no contagion. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wong Choon Mei \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2002\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PANGKOR LAUT, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 6 (Reuters) - Japan's leaders should not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the falling stock market as that would not prevent an "unavoidable" financial crisis from hitting the economy, former financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said on Friday \par \par \uc2  Japan's ex-vice finance minister for international affairs told Reuters an economic crisis was looming for his country as current leaders muddled through, but added that problems were domestic and should not cause damaging ripples around the world. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, whose every word used to be dissected by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers when he was in office, said he expected the yen to trade between 116 and 121 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan had exhausted all possible macroeconomic solutions, adding that only aggressive, structural reforms targeted at cutting its huge debts could help. \par \par \uc2  "A financial crisis is unavoidable," Sakakibara said in an interview on the Malaysian resort island of Pangkor Laut, where he is due to address an international conference. \par \par \uc2  "It could happen this month, three to six months time, but sooner or later it will happen," he added. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, who had a reputation for aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he was at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said existing leaders lacked the resolve to push through necessary reforms. \par \par \uc2  "Only structural reforms can help, but the current leadership does not have the resolve. They are just muddling along," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We can solve it ourselves but the leaders must have the power and will to break out of the existing mould." \par \par \uc2  HARD TO SAY WHICH THE WEAKER \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said it was difficult to say whether the yen or the dollar was weaker, and expected the yen to stay ranged until "the market decides". \par \par \uc2  "It is funny, both are weak and nobody is quite sure which is worse," he said. \par \par \uc2  "But among the majors, the euro has the most strength because the economy there is still doing alright." \par \par \uc2  So far, little impact has been felt in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, though dealers warned further falls in Japanese equity prices could prompt liquidation of overseas assets by Japanese investors, generating yen buying against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yen was around 118 to the dollar and was changing hands at a four-week low against the euro, weakened by a fall in Japan's key Nikkei share average to 19-year trading lows. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei closed down 1.01 percent at 9,129.07. \par \par \uc2  Known as "Mr Yen" because of his reputation for aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Sakakibara is still seen as an influential figure in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  He left the ministry in 1999 and is now a professor at Keio University. \par \par \uc2  MUDDLING THROUGH \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Japan's economy was in a mess and that short-term measures to shore up the stock market were futile. \par \par \uc2  While strong exports helped Japan's economy grow 0.5 percent in the April-June quarter, consumer spending continues to languish, depressed by years of profit-crushing deflation. \par \par \uc2  Beleaguered Japanese firms are still more ready to fire rather than hire, and unemployment stands at 5.4 percent, just shy of its post-war high. \par \par \uc2  "Japan has already exhausted all macro policies," he said, adding that there were no longer any effective weapons to combat the long-lived problem of deflation. \par \par \uc2  He also said investors should not hope for a reprise of February, when government steps prised the market off 18-year lows in the face of a fiscal year-end banking crisis. \par \par \uc2  "They have already done it once, you can only do it once." \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has now lost more than three-quarters of its value since 1989 when Tokyo's shares were worth more than the rest of the world's equity markets combined, and a Reuters poll of eight analysts last week suggested it could bottom out between 8,400 and 9,000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020906dy9600jgg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
