\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia To Maintain Loose Monetary Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2001\par 10:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Malaysia will maintain its loose monetary policy as a means to boost economic growth, the finance ministry said Friday in its annual 2001/2002 economic report. \par \par \uc2  The annual economic report, released in conjunction with the budget presentation to parliament, noted such a stance is needed to "mitigate the slack external demand," given the expected prolonged economic slowdown in the U.S. and Japan. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead to 2002, the finance ministry said monetary policy "will continue to remain accommodative" as a way to complement the government's fiscal stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  Global easing of interest rates has given the country greater flexibility for maintaining a loose monetary stance, the report noted. \par \par \uc2  In tandem with rate cuts around the world following the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S., Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept. 20 lowered its key three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to 5% from 5.5%, the first cut in more than two years. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said last month that any further monetary measures this year would be "less visible" than the interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Banks use the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to calculate their base lending rates, or prime rates. The move was accompanied by a lowering of deposit rates to help banks cope with an expected margin squeeze. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia has maintained a regime of low interest rates since the Asian financial crisis to sustain economic recovery. So far, low inflation hasn't pressured Bank Negara to alter that stance. \par \par \uc2  The economic report projects average annual inflation of just 1.3% for this year, and the rate is likely to remain benign at below 2% in 2002. Nevertheless, the report cautioned external pressures following the events on Sept. 11 could cause consumer prices to edge upward next year. \par \par \uc2  "The recent hike in freight rates, insurance premiums and other transport-related costs is of concern, as they may translate into higher costs," the report said. \par \par \uc2  Also, higher domestic demand could "generate inflationary pressures" if supply shortages, particularly for food, aren't resolved. \par \par \uc2  "Given these risks, the government will remain vigilant to take the necessary measures in containing inflation," the report said, adding that these include boosting local food production and reducing the cost of doing business. \par \par \uc2  Turning to the ringgit peg, the economic report praised the fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system for providing stability during this period of global economic uncertainty, implying the peg won't be changed any time soon. Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad has repeatedly stated there are no grounds for Malaysia to remove or to alter the peg. \par \par \uc2  "The ringgit peg continues to provide an environment of stability and predictability to facilitate economic activities," said the finance ministry. Mahathir also holds the finance portfolio, after announcing the retirement of his former minister Daim Zainuddin in early June. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia pegged the ringgit at 3.80 to the dollar in September 1998 in the midst of the Asian economic crisis as part of its capital controls to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The government hasn't altered the level of the peg, despite calls to do so earlier this year when regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened. \par \par \uc2  "The ringgit peg will be closely monitored against both domestic and external developments to ensure that it remains consistent with macroeconomic fundamentals," the report said, adding that since the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S., the ringgit has depreciated by 0.2% to 3% against major and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "What should give us confidence regarding the ringgit peg...is the acceptance of the ringgit value by all parties, and (that) there is no black market in the ringgit," Mahathir said in his preface to the report. \par \par \uc2  -By Tara Patel, Dow Jones Newswires; 60-3-2692-5254; Tara.Patel@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011019dxaj000gg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
