\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Malaysia sees inflation at 1.8 pct in 2000. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabyasachi Mitra \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2000\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANDAR SERI BEGAWAN, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Malaysia's inflation rate is likely to dip to about 1.8 percent in 2000 from 2.8 percent last year, despite a proposed hike in petroleum prices, Deputy Finance Minister Shafie Salleh said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Shafie told Reuters in an interview the subdued inflation outlook would help banks to keep lending rates at low levels in the near-term. \par \par \uc2  "Based on present trends, it is anticipated that the inflation rate would be about 1.8 percent in 2000 as a whole, considering that excess capacity still exists in the economy and monetary policy continues to be accommodative to support economic growth," he said. \par \par \uc2  Shafie is leading the Malaysian delegation at the seventh APEC finance ministers meeting in the tiny oil-rich sultanate of Brunei. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's inflation rate, measured by the consumer price index, rose by just 1.5 percent year-on-year during January-July, compared with 3.1 percent in the year-ago period. \par \par \uc2  The government plans to increase the price of petroleum for the first time in nearly two decades. The move has raised concerns about the impact on overall prices. \par \par \uc2  But the minister said an increase in petroleum's price would have a minimal impact on the inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  LOW RATES TO REMAIN \par \par \uc2  "As monetary policy will continue to remain accommodative to firmly support growth, it is likely that the 3-month BNM (Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate will remain at the current 5.5 percent," Shafie said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara's three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is used by banks and financial institutions as a benchmark to set their prime lending rates. \par \par \uc2  Last month, Malaysian commercial banks and financial institutions raised deposit rates by 25 basis points, fuelling fears of a hike in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  But Shafie allayed such fears saying the banks had sufficient money available to ease any upward pressure on lending rates. \par \par \uc2  "Given the ample liquidity emanating from both domestic loan growth and current account surplus in the balance of payments, banks will have sufficient funds to lend without pressure for lending rates to increase," he said. \par \par \uc2  BUOYANT GROWTH \par \par \uc2  Shafie said the economy, spurred by strong exports and buoyant domestic demand, was likely to expand more than the official estimate of 5.8 percent in the current year. \par \par \uc2  "While official estimates stand at 5.8 percent, growth in GDP is expected to be higher, given that growth in the first half was 10.3 percent. Private analysts estimate growth at between 6.0-8.0 percent," he said. \par \par \uc2  A recent Reuters poll forecast the economy to grow by 8.6 percent in 2000 and moderate to 6.2 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's gross domestic product (GDP) grew by 8.8 percent year-on-year in the second quarter of 2000 against 11.9 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Shafie said Malaysia's economic recovery was now broad-based and domestic demand was gathering steam in response to the government's expansionary fiscal and monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  RINGGIT PEG TO STAY \par \par \uc2  He said there was no need to adjust the two-year-old ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, which was in line with economic fundamentals and benefitted the economy. \par \par \uc2  "Currently, the degree of undervaluation is very small and the stable exchange rate has a positive impact on economic activity," he said. \par \par \uc2  "However, if circumstances occur which cause a gross misalignment of the ringgit exchange rate, then Bank Negara Malaysia will, of course respond accordingly." \par \par \uc2  Malaysia imposed capital controls and pegged its ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 3.8 to the U.S. dollar in September, 1998, to stem the outflow of short-term capital during the Asian economic crisis in 1997-98. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9a08kxl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
