\par\par \b \uc2  Tame Malaysia inflation mirrors weak domestic demand. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabyasachi Mitra \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 1999\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Nov 15 (Reuters) - Malaysia on Monday announced a benign inflation rate which analysts said indicated weak domestic demand and excess capacity in many industrial sectors. \par \par \uc2  The Statistics Department said consumer prices were 2.1 percent higher in October over the same month in 1998. The consumer price index was unchanged at 118.8 points in October from the preceding month. \par \par \uc2  Average prices in the first 10 months of the year rose 2.9 percent compared with the same period a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  "The two main groups, food and gross rent, fuel and power together accounted for 75.6 percent of the overall increase recorded in the current period," the Statistics Department said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  WEAK DEMAND CAPS INFLATION \par \par \uc2  "Domestic demand is weak and growth is mainly due to external demand," said Ng Bok Eng, senior economist at Daiwa Research Institute of Research in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "It is also partly because of the base factor. Last year, at this point, it was higher because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis effect." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that strong foreign demand for imports, mainly electrical and electronic goods, sparked the recent recovery. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia expects gross domestic product to expand 4.3 percent in 1999 and 5.0 percent in 2000 after shrinking 7.5 percent in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "There is lot of slack in the economy and people are still cautious on spending," said an economist at a Malaysian bank. \par \par \uc2  Eddie Lee, regional economist at Vickers Ballas in Singapore, said demand in many sectors had started picking up but was still below the buoyancy witnessed before the Asian financial crisis erupted in mid-1997. \par \par \uc2  Analysts forecast a low inflation rate over the next six months and expected it to average around 3.0 percent in 1999. \par \par \uc2  BALANCING INTERESTS OF SAVERS AND BORROWERS \par \par \uc2  Subdued inflation and excess liquidity provided the government with the leeway to lower interest rates, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's ruling coalition, facing its toughest electoral battle in four decades, was keen to balance the interests of savers and borrowers. \par \par \uc2  The government recently unveiled plans to issue 2.0 billion ringgit worth of savings bonds to people above 55 and not employed on a full time basis to ease the impact of low bank deposit interest rates on their incomes. \par \par \uc2  "Interest rates are very, very low. There has to be a balance between savers and borrowers," Ng said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara has brought the key \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate down in 50 basis point stages, from 7.0 percent in April to 5.5 percent. The last cut came on August 9. ($1 = 3.8 ringgit) ((Kuala Lumpur newsroom +60 3 2275 6846, fax +60 2 232 6752, kuala.lumpur@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbf09z2d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
