\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Target Of 8% Bank Loan Growth Out Of Reach \b0\par\par\uc2  By Joseph Edwin \par \uc2 1,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 1999\par 10:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Despite prodding from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Malaysian banks aren't likely to achieve the 8.0% growth in lending this year that has been targeted by the government, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Several positive factors, such as government-sponsored home-ownership campaigns, a possible further interest rate cut, and the recent surge in Malaysian share prices, haven't encouraged banks and consumers to step up the pace of new loans by that much. Analysts say loan growth of around 4.0% is the most that the banks are likely to achieve this year. \par \par \uc2  Banks remain reluctant to lend and consumers remain reluctant to borrow until the economy shows more definite signs of a recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The economy has to start going before loan demand comes in," says Pong Teng Siew, assistant research manager at Kuala Lumpur based securities firm KAF Seagroatt & Campbell Sdn. Bhd. \par \par \uc2  Even so, Malaysia should still be able to achieve its target of 1.0% growth in gross domestic product this year, analysts said. Robust exports will make up for any shortfall in bank lending, providing enough of a boost to the general economy. \par \par \uc2  But Malaysian officials continue to insist that a revival in loan growth is both necessary and achievable. Bank Negara Malaysia Governor Ali Abul Hassan Sulaiman said last Tuesday he is confident stricter banking rules announced in April coupled with current low interest rates will spur fresh lending to meet the government's 8.0% growth target by the year end. He noted that some local banks have achieved loan growth rates of around 20% so far this year. \par \par \uc2  He also said 8.0% lending growth is imperative for the country's gross domestic product to expand 1% this year. \par \par \uc2  But government officials have had a poor record of getting domestic banks to increase their lending, since the crisis that erupted after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations of July 1997. \par \par \uc2  At the end of March, the latest figure available, total loans in Malaysia's banking system rose only 1.3% year-on-year. Compared to February, loans grew at a measly 0.1%. And despite an 8.0% loans growth target for 1998, lending contracted 1.8% year-on-year. In comparison, lending grew 21.3% in 1997 and 26.3% in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said banks will continue to lend cautiously as long as they remain mired in nonperforming loans. \par \par \uc2  "Banks are still in the midst of restructuring after the recent (regional financial) crisis. They are wary of over-extending themselves," says Sani Hamid, regional economist at Standard & Poor's MMS in Singapore. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1999 Loans Growth Seen Up To 4%, Lower Than 8% Target\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A reduction in bad loans is expected to encourage lending this year,\par but not significantly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By June, Malaysia's asset management company, Danaharta Nasional Bhd.\par is expected to reach its target of absorbing up to MYR40.0 billion from\par an estimated MYR100.0 billion in bad loans from the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Danaharta was set up last June to buy bad loans from Malaysia's banks,\par and has since taken over MYR8.0 billion worth of bad loans from 39\par financial institutions, and is managing another MYR11.0 billion for two\par banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But even with fewer problem loans, banks will remain reluctant to lend\par as long as the economic recovery is tentative. The economy remains\par plagued by oversupply in the manufacturing, property and services\par sectors, holding back the pace of recovery and making banks cautious\par about new lending, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It will take at least a year before these problems are resolved,\par analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The recent rally in the stock market will help to bolster consumer\par demand in Malaysia, but won't be enough to raise lending levels to the\par government target.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spectacular rally in the Malaysian stock market in the last two\par months has pushed up the benchmark Kuala Lumpur Composite Index to\par 743.34 points Monday, from only 498.57 on March 30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even a second home-ownership campaign, to be launched in Kuala Lumpur\par this Thursday, won't propel many Malaysians to apply for loans, analysts\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many prospective house buyers have already made a home purchase during\par the first campaign in January, when property sales reached MYR3.79\par billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A poor outlook due to an oversupply in residential properties is also\par expected to keep away buyers, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We have already seen the bulk of house buyers during the first\par campaign. Whether they can entice more this time around is left to be\par seen," said Sani at MMS.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GDP Target Can Be Achieved on Sustained Trade Surplus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also, there will be fewer buyers this time around since many people\par believe they should wait until the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cuts interest rates\par further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And a further reduction in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate is\par unlikely to sharply boost lending activities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last week, junior finance minister Mustapa Mohamed said in Tokyo that\par interest rates in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not bottomed out, indicating that an\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate cut was imminent. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate is used by\par commercial banks to compute their prime rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last cut its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate - the rate at which it\par borrows or lends in the interbank market - to 6.00% from 6.50% on May 3,\par in line with expectations of a lower inflation rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Far more important for loans than rates at this point are job\par security, business confidence, consumer sentiment and political\par stability, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the increase in layoffs has eased, thousands of Malaysian\par employees are still retrenched each month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business confidence remains skittish as listed companies continue to\par post losses.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And political concerns now focus on long-serving Prime Minister\par Mahathir Mohamad's successor and when the 73-year-old Mahathir will step\par down.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mahathir has said recently that his deputy Abdullah Ahmad Badawi will\par succeed him within the next four years, but analysts say Abdullah has\par yet to prove himself as capable leader and lacks the vision and tenacity\par of his boss.\par   -By Joseph Edwin, 603 292 5254; jedwin@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 25-05-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0821GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  2558 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv5p03ybc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\