\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Malaysia sees dim light at end of tunnel. \b0\par\par\uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 13:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, March 31 (Reuters) - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday delivered a cautiously upbeat outlook for 1999, predicting the economy will recover modestly this year from Asia's financial crisis and its first recession in 13 years. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara said gross domestic product is expected to expand by one percent in 1999 after contracting by 6.7 percent last year - the worst battering since 1985. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were split over whether the economy will grow even modestly this year but said authorities had scope to push down interest rates and prime the spending pump to stimulate growth. \par \par \uc2  They also said that whatever Malaysia does, its decision last year to slap controls on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows has left a bad taste in foreign investors' mouths and will make it more difficult for the nation to attract overseas investment. \par \par \uc2  "Malaysia has to make up its mind whether it wants to take part in this global economy," Raymond Lim, group chief economist with ABN Amro Asia in Singapore, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "As long as it has doubts and it is arrogant in a vociferous manner, it will continue to put off foreign capital and that will definitely affect Malaysia's growth as the region recovers." \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara said economic growth will be marginal in the first half of 1999 in view of high inventory build-up since early last year, then would pick up in the last six months. \par \par \uc2  "There are indications that the trough in the growth cycle has been reached in the third quarter of 1998 and prospects are for a gradual recovery in 1999," Bank Negara governor Ali Abul Hassan Sulaiman said in the foreword to the 291-page report. \par \par \uc2  Barring a global economic setback, the bank said GDP growth could inch closer to two percent in 1999 as dozens of banks and companies hit by the crisis are overhauled. \par \par \uc2  But it said the prospects for continued export growth were uncertain and the main growth engine would be domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  "Fewer people dispute that Malaysia is doing something right," said Margaret Chin-Wolf, senior fund manager at Pauli Investment Management in Singapore. "They are tackling their problems." \par \par \uc2  Tan Chong Koay, chief executive of Pheim Asset Management Sdn Bhd in Kuala Lumpur said: "The worst is over." \par \par \uc2  But Geoffrey Dennis, Deutsche Bank's global emerging markets equity strategist, said Malaysia was being overly optimistic in projecting one percent GDP growth in 1999. \par \par \uc2  "We think one percent is still somewhat optimistic. We're arguing growth will probably be negative. Malaysia's not in the forefront of the recovery stories in Asia at this point." \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed Malaysia, with a burgeoning current account surplus and subdued inflation, could continue to cut interest rates to help stimulate growth. \par \par \uc2  "What was a surprise is that they did not lower interest rates," said C.T. Yeo, country treasurer for Bank of America Malaysia Bhd in Kuala Lumpur. \par \par \uc2  "We thought they should have taken the opportunity to lower the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate down to six percent from seven percent." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposed capital controls and fixed its ringgit exchange rate in September against a backdrop of mounting economic and political tension. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara governor Ali gave a robust defence of the capital controls, saying they had provided the stability needed to ensure recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Malaysia is a separate case until they free those capital controls up," Dennis said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv3v05m5m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
