\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Short-Term Money Market Marginally Lower Early \b0\par\par\uc2 243 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1998\par 04:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Malaysian short-term interest rates are easing slightly mid-morning Tuesday in thin trading activities. \par \par \uc2  The one-week, two-week and three-week term rates stand at around 5.5% to 5.8%, compared with 5.5% to 6.0% late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate, which indicates short-term liquidity conditions is expected to be around 5% to 5.5%, compared with 5% at the close Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very quiet," said a money market dealer. \par \par \uc2  Even the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced early Tuesday that it had forecast an aggregate liquidity surplus of 1.44 billion ringgit (MYR) ($1=MYR3.80) in the interbank system for the day but it didn't borrow any money from the system as it did Monday. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark three-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Offered Rate is lower at 6.53%, compared with 6.54% Monday. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates have been softening since selected capital controls came into effect at the beginning of September. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The policy was crafted to allow Malaysia's economic planners to pump\par more liquidity into the recession-hit economy without undermining the\par stability of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Klibor Fixing          Tuesday        Monday\par    1-Month                6.23%          6.26%\par    3-Month                6.53%          6.54%\par    6-Month          