\par\par \b \uc2  HK welcomes stable markets, but wants results. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tan Ee Lyn \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1998\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Hong Kong residents, shell shocked by deflated stock and property values, high interest rates and rising unemployment, on Tuesday welcomed the promise of relief from new government measures to stabilise local markets. \par \par \uc2  But political analysts warned that the territory's leaders will face mounting public pressure if the new regulations and a costly stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fail to produce results. \par \par \uc2  "Any kind of draconian measure will be accepted because Hong Kong people are infused with the desire to avoid an economic meltdown," said political observer Lau Siu-kai. \par \par \uc2  "But in the long-term, people will want results. How can you put in billions of dollars and not see results?" said Lau, who is also a sociology professor at the Chinese University. \par \par \uc2  Starting in mid-August, the government has spent more than HK$100 billion (US$12.9 billion) on stock and futures to foil market manipulators it accused of attacking the local dollar to drive interest rates up and stock prices down to take profits on short stock positions. \par \par \uc2  Since then, Hong Kong's struggle with economic recession has become intertwined with the government's fight against market speculators and its defence of the territory's U.S. dollar-linked exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Local media have described the government's actions as a "war" with international hedge funds who are preying on Hong Kong's open economy and regional economic instability. \par \par \uc2  Editorial cartoons and even front-page newspaper graphics have depicted Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  boss Joseph Yam and other officials in sword fights with dark-suited figures labeled "speculators" in defence of the dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  Discussion of the government's measures and the strength of the dollar peg - viewed as unimpeachable just a few months ago - now dominate talk among business people and non-investors alike. \par \par \uc2  Residents have largely voiced support for the actions, saying they may prevent the territory from suffering the same type of economic meltdown plaguing neighbouring countries, such as Thailand, Indonesia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary. Speculators who come here can create trouble," said retiree Lo Kam-seng, 55. "If the government doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the stock market will fall even more." \par \par \uc2  Lo added that during "normal times," \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be good for the economy. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA announced over the weekend seven measures to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board and on Monday the government unveiled a 30-point plan to tighten rules and improve transparency in stock and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  But while many approved of the measures, the applause was muted as some wondered if Hong Kong, which has prided itself as one of the world's most open economies, might perhaps be paying too high a cost, both monetarily and in damage to its free-market reputation. \par \par \uc2  "The money would have been better spent on education, welfare or to lower the unemployment rate where more people would stand to gain," said Chris Chan, a working mother. \par \par \uc2  Political analyst Sonny Lo, an associate professor of politics at the University of Hong Kong, said there would be more domestic pressure on leader Tung Chee-hwa's administration after this spate with foreign speculators. \par \par \uc2  "This issue has polarised Hong Kong society, with some agreeing that fighting speculators is right and must cost money, while the other camp wants the government to concentrate on domestic issues, such as welfare," Lo said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9800ynt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
