\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Malaysia imposes  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  controls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nelson Graves \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Sept 1 (Reuters) - Malaysia slapped controls on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade on Tuesday to shield the battered ringgit against global instability and speculators, long blamed by Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad for Asia's financial woes. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara, said the controls would insulate Malaysia's recession-hit economy from the global financial "crisis", allowing cuts in interest rates without affecting the business of traders and investors. \par \par \uc2  "The overriding objective of the new measures is to regain monetary independence and insulate the Malaysian economy from the prospects of further deterioration in the world economic and financial environment," Bank Negara said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "In the process, the nation would be adequately prepared to minimise the impact of a possible global economic crisis and a breakdown in the international financial system." \par \par \uc2  The range of measures threw the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market into confusion but the ringgit strengthened dramatically, rising to about four per U.S. dollar at 0614 GMT from 4.18 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir, who has repeatedly pointed a finger of blame at speculators for Asia's troubles, was set to take questions from a panel of three interviewers over nationwide television at 3 p.m. (0700 GMT). \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the measures would affect some types of short-term capital flows but current account transactions and foreign direct investment would be unaffected. \par \par \uc2  It said foreigners now needed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approval to convert ringgit into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The measures would limit speculators' ability to deal in the ringgit, which has lost nearly 40 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar since the outbreak of Asia's financial crisis in mid-1997. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the move would virtually put an end to speculative trade in the ringgit but also undermine Malaysia's reputation as having one of the most open economies among developing countries. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, although the ringgit is likely to strengthen as a result of this as speculators are effectively wiped out from the market, Malaysia's efforts in financial liberalisation have experienced a considerable setback," markets consultancy I.D.E.A. said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara's governor and deputy governor, who officials said opposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, submitted their resignations last week, exposing a rift in the government. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that Malaysia was still committed to the free market and the trend towards liberalisation, but there were limits to its commitment. \par \par \uc2  "The benefits of the market can only be realised in an environment of stable and efficient global financial markets," it said, adding that it would withdraw the controls once there was a "discernible normalisation" of the financial markets. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves were the latest example of a government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shield the economy and followed efforts by Hong Kong, Taiwan and Russia to protect their markets. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara chastised the international community for failing to tackle the risks linked to globalised markets. \par \par \uc2  "While arguments have been put forward for emerging economies to undertake economic and financial reforms," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, "of greater urgency is the need to reform the international financial system to better cope with the changed international financial environment." \par \par \uc2  Mahathir has repeatedly railed against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, accusing them of undermining the gains that Malaysia made during a decade of eight percent or more annual economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's economy shrank 6.8 percent between the second quarters of 1997 and 1998. Since it had also fallen 2.8 percent during the year to the first quarter, the economy is now formally in its first recession in 13 years. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9101xcg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
