\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Resignations/Malaysia -2: Zeti As Acting Governor \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 02:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "Unless someone has managed to convince him otherwise, he is leaning to some form of controls," says a Singapore-based economist with close links to Bank Negara. This economist and financial executives say Dr. Mahathir's decision not to attend the Non-Aligned Movement summit meeting in South Africa this week indicates that the 73-year-old prime minister is considering drastic economic measures. \par \par \uc2  It remains unclear what shape exchange controls might take. Many economists say that altering Malaysia's liberal capital policies would hurt a country that relies heavily on global trade and foreign investment, because international investors want to be able to move capital around the world freely. \par \par \uc2  In recent months, Dr. Mahathir and Daim Zainuddin, the minister appointed in June with a brief to get the economy moving, have said Malaysia will pursue an easier monetary policy and government will spend more. But many economists say that route is extremely risky because it puts further pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on an already stretched financial system. \par \par \uc2  "What the country needs is to put its banking system in order and get foreign money into the system," says a Singapore-based commercial banker. "The resignations of two respected government officials just add more uncertainty." \par \par \uc2  Formerly chief executive of the country's largest banking group, Malayan Banking Bhd., Ahmad was appointed \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in 1993 and is widely credited with repairing Bank Negara's sullied reputation after it lost billions of dollars trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the early 1990s. Ahmad won praise from \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking community in recent months for resisting government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the running of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Since the Asian economic crisis erupted a year ago, Ahmad has rebuffed Dr. Mahathir's entreaties to lower interest rates to help companies that couldn't afford to borrow. Instead, with Finance Minister Anwar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor argued that Malaysia needs high interest rates to attract investors to the ringgit and keep a lid on inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The government has never agreed to the idea that we should have high interest rates and dry up credit, because it will cause a contraction, and we have seen it in the second quarter," Dr. Mahathir told reporters on Saturday, referring to the 6.8% contraction in Malaysia's economy in the second quarter after a 2.8% drop in the first. \par \par \uc2  The government appointed the assistant governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Zeti Akhtar Aziz, as acting governor. As the bank's chief economist, Zeti has been a strong proponent of the policies advocated by her predecessor but isn't regarded as being as forceful. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 31-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0004GMT \par \par \uc2  1123 62543 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8v07fcb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
