\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS -  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  calm but stocks surge. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 11:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Aug 20 (Reuters) - A mostly quiet day on the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front in Asian markets but stocks made a splash with \par \par \uc2  another day of strong gains on indexes in Hong Kong, Korea and \par \par \uc2  Malaysia. \par \par \uc2      Both Hong Kong's Hang Seng index and \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's KLSE were \par \par \uc2  benefitting from actual or rumoured government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \par \par \uc2  while bank merger deals helped Korea's KOSPI. \par \par \uc2      However, Tokyo's exchange, the region's largest, closed \par \par \uc2  slightly lower, with the market little impressed by a media \par \par \uc2  report of a government plan to support a merger between the \par \par \uc2  troubled Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd and Sumitomo Trust \par \par \uc2  & Banking Co Ltd, traders said. \par \par \uc2     The Nikkei average had initially suffered profit-taking, \par \par \uc2  triggered by a setback in Wall Street stocks overnight, but the \par \par \uc2  losses were mostly offset later by the yen's surge, they said. \par \par \uc2     The 225-share Nikkei average finished down 14.93 points or \par \par \uc2  0.10 percent at 15,391.41 while the dollar sank more than one \par \par \uc2  yen in late trade. \par \par \uc2       HONG KONG STOCKS END FIRM WITH DOWNSIDE SEEN LIMITED \par \par \uc2      The Hang Seng index closed up 1.57 percent at 7,742.53 \par \par \uc2  after peaking as high as 7,880 in early trade -- its highest \par \par \uc2  level this month. \par \par \uc2      But dealers are concerned the market is being artificially \par \par \uc2  supported by government buying. They say investors are jumping \par \par \uc2  on the bandwagon but nobody knows when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \par \par \uc2  stop. \par \par \uc2      Brokers expect the government to continue squeezing short \par \par \uc2  sellers until the August Hang Seng Index futures expire on Aug \par \par \uc2  28. \par \par \uc2      MALAYSIAN STOCKS GET A BOOST FROM ANWAR \par \par \uc2      In Kuala Lumpur, the KLSE moved from strength to strength \par \par \uc2  as a key resistance at 368 was breached on local buying. \par \par \uc2      Fresh buying was sparked by Deputy Prime Minister Anwar \par \par \uc2  Ibrahim's comments that drastic measures were being planned for \par \par \uc2  the economy. \par \par \uc2      Anwar also expanded on Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's \par \par \uc2  Tuesday comments that the Moslem pilgrims fund could enter the \par \par \uc2  market to buy shares in the local stock market without waiting \par \par \uc2  for a government directive. \par \par \uc2      Anwar said only stocks deemed halal -- acceptable to Islam \par \par \uc2  -- such as companies not dealing in gaming, liquor and tobacco \par \par \uc2  production could be bought. \par \par \uc2      But dealers say the market is becoming sceptical at how \par \par \uc2  long the market can sustain the government-linked rally. \par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE POSTS DROP IN JULY IN NON-OIL DOMESTIC EXPORT \par \par \uc2      Singapore posted weaker July non-oil domestic exports, \par \par \uc2  reflecting deteriorating Asian consumption and slowing demand \par \par \uc2  in key markets. \par \par \uc2      Non-oil domestic exports fell in July by 2.3 percent \par \par \uc2  year-on-year in nominal terms to 7.59 billion Singapore \par \par \uc2  dollars, after rising 6.1 percent in June, according to the \par \par \uc2  Trade and Development Board. \par \par \uc2  Economists and analysts said the July fall was expected \par \par \uc2  because of Asia's economic crisis and oversupply in the global \par \par \uc2  electronics market. \par \par \uc2      Electronics account for 70 percent of Singapore's non-oil \par \par \uc2  domestic exports, and more than 50 percent of manufacturing. \par \par \uc2      TAIWAN CHALKS UP HUGE 2ND QTR CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS \par \par \uc2      Taiwan's current account posted a surplus of $753 million \par \par \uc2  in the second quarter of 1998 compared with a surplus of $1.408 \par \par \uc2  billion in the second quarter of 1997, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2      The second-quarter current account surplus was more than \par \par \uc2  twice as big as analysts expected. A Reuters Poll of economists \par \par \uc2  on August 17 yielded an average forecast of US$311 million. \par \par \uc2      Taiwan's merchandise trade surplus shrank to $2.535 billion \par \par \uc2  in the second quarter, compared with a surplus of $3.612 \par \par \uc2  billion a year ago, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2      The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attributed the year-on-year decline in \par \par \uc2  current account surplus to Southeast Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis \par \par \uc2  eating into the island's exports. \par \par \uc2      Taiwan stocks ended marginally off but were well-supported \par \par \uc2  on continued optimism over the government's planned \par \par \uc2  market-stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2      The Securities and Futures Commission announced yesterday \par \par \uc2  it was drafting proposals to help stabilise the stock market \par \par \uc2  following President Lee Teng-hui's call to ensure investor \par \par \uc2  confidence. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan cabinet adopted a 193.7 billion Taiwan dollar \par \par \uc2  stimulus plan for the 1999-2000 fiscal year on Thursday, but \par \par \uc2  dealers were still cautious after Wall Street's overnight \par \par \uc2  losses. \par \par \uc2      JAKARTA SHARES WEAK AS NEW BANKRUPTCY CODE BECOMES \par \par \uc2  EFFECTIVE \par \par \uc2      Jakarta stocks fell as a new bankruptcy law came into \par \par \uc2  effect. Investors are worried this could be the start of a slew \par \par \uc2  of bankruptcies that might even claim some listed companies. \par \par \uc2      The bankruptcy law is designed to give creditors more power \par \par \uc2  to force debtors to restructure and pay off their debts. Firms \par \par \uc2  that seek bankruptcy protection will have 270 days to \par \par \uc2  restructure and work out their payment plan with creditors. \par \par \uc2  After that, they can be liquidated. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN STOCKS AIDED BY BANK MERGER PLANS \par \par \uc2  South Korea's stock market index, the KOSPI, rose by nearly \par \par \uc2  five percent, helped by a stronger yen and aggressive foreign \par \par \uc2  buying. \par \par \uc2  Blue chips remained bullish on arbitrage buying and bank \par \par \uc2  shares came into the spotlight mainly due to a merger deal \par \par \uc2  between Hana and Boram banks, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Stock Exchange composite stock price index shot \par \par \uc2  up by 4.87 percent, or 14.69 points, to close at 316.59, \par \par \uc2  recovering from the day low of 301.76. Gainers led losers by \par \par \uc2  638 to 155 with 123 stocks going limit up. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors became net buyers for the first time \par \par \uc2  since August 11. The amount of their buying is really big," \par \par \uc2  said Kim Kyung-hack, a market analyst at the Korea Stock \par \par \uc2  Exchange. \par \par \uc2      The exchange said foreign investors bought a net 13.9 \par \par \uc2  billion won and individual investors 29.1 billion. \par \par \uc2      Brokers said heavy buy orders poured in for bank shares as \par \par \uc2  investors believed a wave of bank restructuring was in the \par \par \uc2  offing with the Hana-Boram merger and the Commercial-Hanil bank \par \par \uc2  merger announced last month. \par \par \uc2  The banking sub-index rose 3.96 percent to 76.62 points. \par \par \uc2  A senior official at Hana Bank said the presidents of the \par \par \uc2  two banks agreed on a merger deal Wednesday night and a formal \par \par \uc2  signing of the letter of intent was expected soon. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was also firmer, breaking through the 1,300 \par \par \uc2  level by midday trade. Dealers said dollar selling from Kia \par \par \uc2  Motors and the strong yen helped prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Prospects for the won remained good with the weekend ahead \par \par \uc2  and export negotiations lined up for next week. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Change on the day THU 20 0953GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest..bid...Prev Close......Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit....4.1700.........4.2000.........0.72% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah.....11600.0000.....11500.0000....-0.86% \par \par \uc2   Baht.......41.0900........41.5500........1.12% \par \par \uc2   Peso.......42.5500........42.8000........0.59% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr...1.7475.........1.7500.........0.14% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr.34.5570........34.7000........0.41% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won.1295.0000......1303.0000......0.62% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest..........July '97....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit....4.1700.........2.5200.......-39.57% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah.....11600.0000.....2432.0000....-79.03% \par \par \uc2   Baht.......41.0900........25.9000......-36.97% \par \par \uc2   Peso.......42.5500........26.5000......-37.72% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr...1.7475.........1.4300.......-18.17% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr.34.5570........