\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Yen and yuan worries hurt markets. \b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 10:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Most Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Monday in line with a weaker Japanese yen, which was trading close to 146.5 per dollar after nearing an eight-year low of 146.75 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a test of 146.75 could come soon. \par \par \uc2  Persistent worries about the Chinese yuan also weighed on markets, despite comments over the weekend by China's Jiang Zemin pledging support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG STILL A CONCERN, DESPITE CALMER DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the resiliency of the Hong Kong dollar peg after a series of speculative attacks last week as well as worries about the Chinese yuan were likely to keep the dollar strong against the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was under less pressure on Monday, with forward spreads narrowing and interest rates falling. By mid-afternoon, the three-month forward rate was at 1050/1120 points compared with 1400/1500 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment had improved but they said the local dollar was still susceptible to attack, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, there were worries the Hong Kong government's next defence of the peg would not be so easy. Last week, the government's purchases of Hong Kong dollars were funded with sales of U.S. dollar assets from fiscal accounts, skirting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system and keeping a lid on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  While most Asian stock markets were lower on Monday because of the weakening yen, Hong Kong's blue-chip Hang Seng Index was mildly firmer on the back of easier local interest rates. The index ended up 0.23 percent at 7,034.62. \par \par \uc2  CHINA \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2 , AGAIN \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the Chinese yuan at 8.2800 to the dollar at the close on Monday, little changed from Friday's close. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level. \par \par \uc2  On Sunday, Chinese President Jiang Zemin repeated that Beijing was determined to avoid devaluing the yuan. \par \par \uc2  China's share markets slumped to new lows. Shanghai's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares ended down 3.44 percent at a record-low close of 27.634 points due to falls in domestic A shares. The weaker yen and poor corporate interim results were blamed for the fall. \par \par \uc2  The Shenzhen B share index also closed at a record low, down 3.66 percent at 55.46 points. \par \par \uc2  MALAYSIA CUTS INTEREST RATES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Bank Negara cut its three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by 50 basis points to 10 percent, its second cut in a week. The ringgit eased modestly on the move. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  already had taken an early hit with the market still worried about the state of the country's banking and corporate sectors. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia on Friday announced the creation of a new committee to help companies restructure debt without seeking customary court protection. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit also was hurt by the Kuala Lumpur stock index, which opened 2.5 percent lower on Monday and later hit a new nine-year low. By 0833 GMT, the KLSE index was down 3.13 percent to 352.66, off a low of 351.47. \par \par \uc2  OTHERS ASIANS MIXED \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar took a big hit, falling by nearly two percent on the back of a weaker yen. Trade was quiet, however, with Singapore markets closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was relatively unscathed and down only slightly, with traders looking for it to firm because of the large amount of U.S. dollars which are due to flow into the country. \par \par \uc2  The baht bucked the region's weakening trend, gaining a bit on local demand. But traders said they were awaiting the announcement of a package to help the country's ailing banking sector. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Korean won and the Taiwan dollar were down amid worries over the yen as well as the Hong Kong and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With the exception of Hong Kong's market, most regional stock markets were weaker on Monday, weighed down by softer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02s86\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
