\par\par \b \uc2  Policy makers change course as deflation hits Asia. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Hubbard \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Government policy makers across Asia are steadily changing direction as a new threat emerges to their already embattled economies - the prospect of serious deflation. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-year the authorities have been loosening the tight monetary policy screws in place since the peak of the Asian crisis, and making plans to boost government spending to try to head it off. \par \par \uc2  In taking this new direction, analysts say, governments have been given a green light by the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  "Deflation is the watchword in Asia at this point," said Tim Condon, regional economist at Morgan Stanley in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  No less a figure than Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji openly admitted the existence of deflation in China's economy just this week. \par \par \uc2  "In view of the current situation in which there is deflation, the central government has decided to take more active fiscal policies to raise more capital and strengthen infrastructure investment," Zhu told a local newspaper on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Economists define deflation as a sustained reduction in the general level of prices. It is often, though not always, accompanied by declines in output and employment. \par \par \uc2  This is certainly happening across Asia now and governments appear to have decided that, since exports are not going to provide the needed stimulus to avoid deflation, they have to step in. \par \par \uc2  "It's a question of economic survival," said Chia Woon Khien, head of Asian research at SE Banken in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Over the past few weeks finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors regionwide have been announcing broadly similar measures. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan cut commercial banks' reserve requirements, resulting in a drop in passbook deposit rates by half a percentage point. The move was designed to spur the economy and bolster money market liquidity. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by 50 basis points to 10.5 percent, effective on Monday, leading at least one bank to cut its base lending rate. It had already announced a major fiscal stimulus package amounting to around three percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's Finance Minister Lee Kyu-sung said he planned to lower interest rates steadily and said the country plans to expand fiscal spending to create jobs. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Chatu Mongkol, said this week there was room to stimulate the economy via monetary expansion and lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Thai government is also in meetings with the IMF this week at which it has said it plans to seek permission for a much larger budget deficit. The government wants to double the fiscal deficit to two percent of GDP from a planned one percent target. It's likely the IMF will agree. \par \par \uc2  Even in Singapore's secretive money markets, some dealers say the authorities have been adding liquidity at the short end over the past couple of weeks by selling the Singapore dollar in a bid to get domestic interest rates down. The government announced a $1.2 billion economic stimulus package at the end of June. \par \par \uc2  The only major country not being threatened by deflation at the present time is Indonesia. This week it posted an inflation rate for the year to July of nearly 70 percent, although output and employment are certainly declining. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the switch to expansionary economic policies is inevitable but worry that it may not be enough. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing countries go from extremely tight economic policies to moderately tight ones. But it still may not be sufficient to boost domestic demand," said Morgan Stanley's Condon. \par \par \uc2  Condon also warns that the price for such policies would normally be a further weakening in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates leading to a worsening of the situation for countries with large amounts of foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  But SE Banken's Chia said many of the economic problems that led to the original \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, such as inflexible exchange rate systems and high current account deficits, have now largely disappeared and there is now much less reason for speculators to sell down Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400xje\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
