\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Ringgit/Risks Rise -2: Political Differences \b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1998\par 09:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  'Going forward, we expect the ringgit to strengthen as a result of better regional sentiment,' Goldman Sachs said in its quarterly economic report released earlier this month. But the U.S. investment bank does share some of Supple's concerns about the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  'The crucial local issue is the financial-sector restructuring. Both Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad and Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim are dedicated to protecting depositors. This focus will limit the scope for capital flight,' the report says. But Malaysia's two top officials don't gel so well on the question of protecting Bumiputra, mostly ethnic Malay, shareholders. \par \par \uc2  'The two differ on the treatment of bank shareholders, with the prime minister more willing to bail out prominent Bumiputra businessmen. A continuation of this policy... will likely depress capital inflows and delay our expected appreciation in the ringgit,' Goldman Sachs says. \par \par \uc2  In the past few weeks, that seems to be the case. The ringgit has been firmly tethered to a MYR3.60 to MYR3.80 range, despite some signs that the economy and banking system are in danger. That's largely because of short-term anxiety that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But with foreign reserves at $20.59 billion (equivalent to around three-and-a-half months of imports), analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be aggressively selling down their supply of dollars. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say they'd be fighting a losing battle as sentiment in the market turns more bearish. In February, the Industrial Production Index fell 2.2% year-on-year, forcing a number of economists to seriously consider the possibility of a full recession in 1998. \par \par \uc2  SocGen-Crosby Securities is looking at a possible 1% contraction this year, and Paribas Asia Equity has put out a 2.6% shrinkage estimate. The government, however, is targeting 2%-3% economic growth this year. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 23-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0750GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4n03f97\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
