\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia monetary policy to be kept tight - Negara. \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, March 25 (Reuters) - Bank Negara said on Wednesday that the risks of inflation remain high following the depreciation of the ringgit, and monetary policy will be kept tight this year to check rising prices. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated in its 1997 annual report that authorities considered the ringgit to be "grossly undervalued" against the U.S. dollar, and that it retains the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  It said the bank's policy is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a degree of stability, not to support any specific level of the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  "Although the growth of the Malaysian economy is expected to moderate in 1998, the risks of inflation remain high following the significant depreciation of the ringgit since mid-1997," the report said. \par \par \uc2  "In this environment, monetary policy will remain tight." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said a tight monetary policy was essential to restore macroeconomic stability and promote stability in the domestic financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "The primary focus of monetary poicy in 1998 will, therefore, continue to be directed at containing any rise in inflation, in particular, inflationary expectations that may be generated by the increase in import prices following the ringgit depreciation," it said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara said, however, that monetary policy would have to be flexible given uncertainties in the external environment, including the magnitude and duration of the regional financial crisis, the course of U.S. monetary policy and developments in Japan. \par \par \uc2  "It will not be the intention of policy to create an over-adjustment or correction of the economy," it said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the slowdown in credit growth will allow for an expansion of monetary aggregates that is more consistent with output growth. \par \par \uc2  But credit growth has "moderated at a much faster pace than expected as a result of an uneven expansion in credit growth among the banking institutions", it said. \par \par \uc2  "These developments, if allowed to persist, can precipitate a credit crunch," it said, adding that tight monetary policy needs to be reinforced with policy adjustments to ensure an efficient functioning of the intermediation process. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara said the sharper increases in prices emerging in 1998 "are caused entirely by the depreciation in the ringgit exchange rate" as domestic demand has slowed and is expected to remain subdued. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its exchange rate policy had remained unchanged during the recent turbulence. \par \par \uc2  "No targets are set for the ringgit exchange rate level and market forces will continue to determine the value of the ringgit," it said. \par \par \uc2  "Currently, the ringgit is assessed as being grossly undervalued," it said, adding that the rate has been driven more by changes in market sentiment than economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has depreciated about 30 percent against the U.S. dollar since a regional financial crisis erupted last July. On Wedneday afternoon it was quoted around 3.63/3.64 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  As Malaysia's adjustment measures take effect, "it is envisaged that the exchange rate will adjust to reflect the economic fundamentals of the country". \par \par \uc2  "As in recent years, the option for Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will continue to remain." ((Kuala Lumpur newsroom, +60 3 201-4813 fax +60 3 232-6752, kuala.lumpur.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3p08mda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
