\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Bank Negara keeps interest rates steady. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 10:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, March 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to borrow funds in the money market for the first time in months to check falling interbank rates, suggesting monetary policy will be kept tight. \par \par \uc2  Money market dealers said Bank Negara Malaysia borrowed overnight, one-month and three-month money at 10.0 percent through an agent bank. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a lender since July to help ease market tightness, after its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the ringgit caused a liquidity squeeze. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara at the same time was lending at 11.0 percent on Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  No confirmation was available as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not normally comment on its open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think Negara is trying to keep rates steady. They are borrowing quite a bit of money but lending out a small amount," a dealer with a Malaysian bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market had turned long, and even some finance companies, which were extremely short earlier, were seen lending last week. \par \par \uc2  "There are now more lenders in the market and Bank Negara will have to borrow to prevent rates from coming off," another dealer with a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The one-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Rate (KLIBOR) fell to 10.91 percent on Monday from 11.04 percent at the end of February. The benchmark three-month KLIBOR was at 11.0 percent, also from 11.04 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said persistent market talk of a cut in the liquid assets ratio of banks was also keeping pressure on rates. \par \par \uc2  "Rumours are still circulating in the market that the liquid assets ratio will be cut to 15 percent from 17 percent," said a dealer with a merchant bank. \par \par \uc2  But most dealers and analysts said such a move was unlikely, given the improving liquidity in the market and so soon after a cut in banks' statutory reserve requirement (SRR). \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara in February cut the SRR - or funds required to be kept interest-free with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to 10 percent of total deposits and other liabilities from 13.5 percent, releasing an additional 14 billion ringgit to the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they will cut it. There's no need for such a move," said Sani Hamid, an analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is not that the market is short but that funds are not being channelled properly across the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said foreign banks were basically lenders as recent ringgit buying had saddled them with excess funds. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said monetary policy would be kept tight to contain inflationary pressures and support a recovery in the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's annual inflation rate was 4.4 percent in February, its highest level in five years. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have forecast a consumer price index rise of between five and six percent for Malaysia this year, compared with 2.7 percent in 1997, following the sharp drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has depreciated by about 30 percent against the U.S. dollar since a regional financial crisis erupted last July. But it has rebounded sharply from a historical low of 4.88 to the dollar in early January, and on Monday was around 3.56. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara's move on Monday came a day before Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim was scheduled to announce fiscal policy measures and discuss inflation. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister was quoted by Taiwan's Economic Daily News on Monday as saying Malaysia planned measures to boost the ringgit to three to the dollar. ((Kuala Lumpur newsroom, +60 3 201-4813 fax +60 3 232-6752, kuala.lumpur.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3n08y0g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
