\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Early; Market Lacks Direction \b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 1998\par 03:27\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)-- Southeast Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are\par mixed in early trading on Monday, with sentiment toward the\par regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  neutral in the short term.\par    "The market is directionless," said one trader at a U.S.\par bank in Singapore. "It's all interbank players pushing the\par rates here and there. There is no customer participation at\par all."\par    With the U.S. dollar offered on the topside, and market\par participants still looking for advantageous levels to buy\par the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are likely to trade\par in ranges until driven one way or the other by fresh news,\par say traders.\par    Market players have shrugged off the weekend's news of\par further rioting in Indonesia's provinces, with the rupiah\par (IDR) strengthening slightly since Friday afternoon.\par    At 0215 GMT (9:15 p.m. Sunday EST) the U.S. dollar is\par quoted at IDR9,350, down from IDR9,600. Traders say that for\par the time being, the U.S. dollar's upside is capped firmly by\par offers from IDR9,600 to IDR10,000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Market reaction has also been muted to the weekend's cut\par in the statutory reserve requirement of Malaysian banks from\par 13.5% to 10%. The extra liquidity pumped into the market is\par seen balanced by the hike in Bank Negara \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate to 11% from 10% and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par pledge to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less in the money market.\par    Against the ringgit (MYR), the U.S. dollar is trading at\par MYR3.8450, unchanged from late Friday.\par    The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also at S$1.6626, up from S$1.6522\par on Friday afternoon, although traders say the U.S. dollar is\par heavily bid at S$1.6650.\par    Against the baht (THB) the U.S. dollar has dropped to\par THB47.75 from THB48.10 late Friday, with traders saying the\par U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to look soft.\par    -By Tom Holland;65 421 4800;tom.holland@djmarkets.com.sg\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  02-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2127EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du2900ahf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
