\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Malaysian ringgit soars, more upside seen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1998\par 05:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Jan 14 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit rallied against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday on offshore and interbank dollar selling, with dealers and analysts saying they expect more gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent selloff. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit jumped to 4.3000 to the dollar early on Wednesday against 4.6050 in Kuala Lumpur late on Tuesday. It was trading around 4.3600/4100 a dollar at 0330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S., European and Japanese banks were seen unwinding their long dollar positions, triggering stop-loss dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  "The market appears to be squaring out of long dollar positions," said a dealer with a European bank in Singapore. "We may be in for a sharp retracement after recent overshooting." \par \par \uc2  The ringgit had fallen about 20 percent last week to a record low of 4.8800 to the dollar, dragged down by a sliding Indonesian rupiah. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented it from going lower. \par \par \uc2  "While a mad rush into Malaysian assets is not expected, a 30 to 40 percent adjustment to the overshooting seen recently is a safe bet," said Sani Hamid, emerging markets analyst at Standard and Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said recent visits of U.S. and International Monetary Fund (IMF) officials to Indonesia and Malaysia may have restored calm in the regional financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "There is the possibility that we have seen the bottom in Asian bourses and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Sani said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost over 40 percent of its value against the dollar since July last year when Bank Negara, after an initial defence, allowed the ringgit to depreciate under the pressure of selling by hedge funds and speculators. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was also helped by Tuesday's remarks from U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Summers, who met Malaysian Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials during a brief stopover in Kuala Lumpur, said he was gratified by Malaysia's commitment to take new economic measures if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "A number of steps taken in recent months have been strong ones," he said, adding that Malaysia had "demonstrated a clear commitment to take further steps if those become necessary". \par \par \uc2  In response to weakened economic growth, Malaysia has cut spending, delayed a slew of infrastructure projects and reduced imports. It is also curbing bank lending. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a report by U.S. investment bank Morgan Stanley Dean Witter suggesting a bottom may be near for Asian markets also discouraged holding of long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "If there is a perfect remedy for investors' confidence, it is such positive reports, as these are the people with large funds to put their money where their mouths are," Sani said. \par \par \uc2  With the present situation indicating that the worst may be over, the market could be looking for a consolidative range for dollar/ringgit, dealers and analyst said. \par \par \uc2  "The ringgit should stay above four (to the dollar) for the time being," the European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  -- Kuala Lumpur Newsroom (603)201 4813; Fax (603)232 6752 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: kuala.lumpur.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1e07z7d\par }\par 